# HR Documents Library — Supabase read path (public.doclib_* views, RLS read-only).
# The anon key is publishable by design, but PostgREST exposes the entire
# `public` schema it's granted on — not just the doclib_* demo views — so
# treat it as a project-wide credential, not a scoped one. Without these vars
# the app serves the identical bundled fixtures.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_your_key_here

# Google Analytics 4 (marketing analytics, optional). Public client-side
# measurement ID; the consent banner is the real gate. Without this var the
# GA4 loader is inert and no script is loaded. Set in the build environment
# (Vercel project settings, GitHub Actions vars, or local .env).
# VITE_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-...

# SEO / prerendering (all optional — see docs/SEO_GEO_IMPLEMENTATION.md).
# VITE_SITE_ORIGIN overrides the canonical origin baked into every canonical
# URL, hreflang link, sitemap entry, and JSON-LD @id at build time. Leave it
# unset in production: the default is https://dutiva.ca (src/seo/site.ts).
# VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://dutiva.ca
#
# Search-engine ownership verification (public tokens, not secrets — but keep
# them out of the repo so each deployment declares its own). When set at
# build time, scripts/prerender.mjs injects the corresponding meta tag into
# every prerendered page.
# GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=...   # <meta name="google-site-verification">
# BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=...     # <meta name="msvalidate.01">

# Stripe billing (Pricing page checkout + billing portal + webhook, all under
# supabase/functions/). Server-side secrets only — never prefix these with
# VITE_ or they'd be bundled into the public client. Without them the
# create-checkout-session / create-portal-session / stripe-webhook functions
# respond 503 "not configured" rather than failing hard; an @dutiva.ca
# account still bypasses the paywall with none of this set (see
# src/lib/billing/adminAccess.ts).
# SITE_URL=https://dutiva.ca                     # apex origin for Stripe success/cancel/return URLs (not www — vercel.json redirects www away)
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...                  # Stripe secret key
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...                # Stripe webhook signing secret (stripe-webhook function)
# STRIPE_PRICE_STARTER_MONTHLY=price_...         # Starter plan monthly price ID
# STRIPE_PRICE_GROWTH_MONTHLY=price_...          # Growth plan monthly price ID
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=price_...             # Professional plan monthly price ID
# Annual price IDs. Create these in Stripe as yearly recurring prices charging
# 10 months' worth per year (ANNUAL_MONTHS_BILLED in src/config/plans.ts — the
# "two months free" convention the /pricing toggle displays). Both the checkout
# function and the webhook's price lookup read them; with any of them unset an
# annual checkout fails with a 503 rather than quietly billing monthly.
# STRIPE_PRICE_STARTER_ANNUAL=price_...          # Starter plan annual price ID
# STRIPE_PRICE_GROWTH_ANNUAL=price_...           # Growth plan annual price ID
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_ANNUAL=price_...              # Professional plan annual price ID

# Beta AI usage guardrails (docs/AI_USAGE_STRATEGY.md §7). All optional — the
# defaults in supabase/functions/_shared/aiUsage.ts already apply, and these
# only exist so the beta ceilings can be tuned by setting a Supabase function
# secret instead of shipping a deploy. Server-side only (never prefix VITE_).
# Anything non-numeric or <= 0 is ignored in favour of the default.
# AI_DAILY_REQUEST_LIMIT=120           # per user, rolling 24h, shared by the Advisor + support helper
# AI_DAILY_TOKEN_LIMIT=250000          # per user, rolling 24h — bounds cost when request count doesn't
# AI_PLATFORM_DAILY_LIMIT=2000         # whole project, rolling 24h — the beta-wide stop
# AI_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS=300          # burst window applied to both surfaces
# AI_BURST_LIMIT_CHAT=10               # advisor-chat calls allowed per burst window, per user
# AI_BURST_LIMIT_SUPPORT=6             # support-firstline calls allowed per burst window, per user

# Customer support system. The config is code (src/config/support.ts) and the
# schema is migrations 0014/0015 — the app needs NONE of these to run. The vars
# below are read only by server-side Supabase functions (never prefix VITE_).
# See docs/SUPPORT_ARCHITECTURE.md + docs/SUPPORT_RUNBOOK.md.
#
# Transactional email (the support-notify send worker drains the outbox). With
# no API key set, the worker is a safe no-op: notifications stay `pending` and
# nothing is sent, so wiring the key later flushes the backlog.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...                # Resend API key — consumed by support-notify.
#                                       # SUPPORT_EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY is the
#                                       # provider-agnostic fallback name (either works).
# SUPPORT_EMAIL_FROM=Dutiva Support <support@dutiva.ca>  # verified Resend sender (defaults to this)
# SUPPORT_NOTIFY_SECRET=...             # shared secret the scheduler sends as x-notify-secret;
#                                       # REQUIRED once a provider key is set (worker fails closed otherwise)
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...       # Svix signing secret for the resend-webhook function
#                                       # (delivery/bounce events). Without it the webhook fails
#                                       # closed (503) — it never accepts unsigned events.
# PUBLIC_INTAKE_SALT=...                # salt for hashing IP/email in the public-intake rate limiter
#                                       # (create-public-support-ticket); falls back to SUPPORT_NOTIFY_SECRET
#
# CAPTCHA on the public intake — the human check behind the honeypot and the
# rate limits. Cloudflare Turnstile (default) or hCaptcha; they share one
# siteverify shape. Set the two keys TOGETHER: with no CAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY the
# function skips verification entirely (how it ships), and once the secret is
# set a missing or bad token is a hard 403 — so shipping the secret without the
# site key would lock every real customer out of the Contact form.
# CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=turnstile            # turnstile (default) | hcaptcha
# CAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY=...                # server-side secret — consumed by create-public-support-ticket
# VITE_CAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=...             # public site key — bundled into the client on purpose
# VITE_CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=turnstile       # must match CAPTCHA_PROVIDER
# SUPPORT_OPERATOR_EMAIL=support@dutiva.ca  # where operator alerts are addressed (defaults to support@dutiva.ca)
# SUPPORT_INBOUND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...    # verify inbound email -> ticket webhooks
#
# Attachment malware scanning (support-attachment-scan worker, migration 0038).
# The worker POSTs {url, file_name, mime_type, size_bytes, reference} to the
# scan URL — a 5-minute signed URL for the object, never ticket content — and
# expects {"status":"clean"|"infected"|"unsupported"} back (the boolean
# `infected`/`clean` shapes and a bare "OK"/"FOUND" body are accepted too).
# With no URL set the worker is a safe no-op: rows stay `pending` and downloads
# are unaffected, so wiring a scanner later scans the backlog. Once the URL IS
# set, `sign` refuses any attachment that has not come back clean.
# SUPPORT_ATTACHMENT_SCAN_URL=...       # malware-scan hook -> support_attachments.scan_status
# SUPPORT_ATTACHMENT_SCAN_KEY=...       # sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to the scan hook
# STATUS_PAGE_API_URL=...               # public status provider feed for the branded /status route

# Export protection (docs/EXPORT_PROTECTION.md). The record-export edge
# function meters exports per account via claim_export_slot (migration 0033)
# and writes the export_events audit trail. Ceilings are env-overridable so
# tuning is a secret change, not a deploy:
# EXPORT_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS=300       # burst window (default 300)
# EXPORT_BURST_LIMIT=10                 # exports per burst window (default 10)
# EXPORT_DAILY_LIMIT=80                 # exports per rolling 24h (default 80)

# Client error reporting (docs/ERROR_REPORTING.md). The browser reporter
# (src/lib/errorReporting) posts privacy-scrubbed crash reports to the
# report-error edge function via a keepalive fetch with credentials omitted (no
# cookies), which stores them in public.client_error_reports (migration 0019). It
# reuses the app's existing VITE_SUPABASE_URL to locate the endpoint and is inert
# unless VERCEL_ENV is 'production' or 'preview' — nothing is sent in dev or
# tests. Deploy report-error with `verify_jwt` off (like resend-webhook) so the
# request reaches it with no auth header. It stores reports via the
# ingest_client_error_report() RPC (atomic per-IP rate limit).
# The source IP is only ever used to rate-limit and is keyed with HMAC under a
# REQUIRED secret pepper — the function fails closed without it:
# ERROR_REPORT_SALT=...                 # REQUIRED pepper for the rate-limit IP HMAC
#                                       # (falls back to SUPPORT_NOTIFY_SECRET)
